.TH grep "" "Command"
.SH NAME
grep \- pattern search
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgrep [\-abchlnsvxy] [\fIpattern\fB] [\fIfile ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
grep -- pattern search
Usage:	grep [ option ... ] [ pattern ] [ file ... ]
Options:
	-a	Extra metacharacters supported ('(...)', '|', '+', and '?')
	-b	Each output line has the block number of the match
	-c	Print only a count of the number of matching lines
	-e	The next argument is the pattern
	-f	The next argument is a file containing one pattern per line
	-h	Suppress the printing of file names on matched lines
	-l	Print only the names of files containing matches
	-n	Print line number of file with each matched line output
	-s	Suppress output, just return status
	-v	Negate the sense of the match
	-x	Exact match (regular expression metacharacters are ordinary)
	-y	Lower-case letters in the pattern match upper- and lower-case
The 'pattern' is a regular expression roughly like that found in 'ed'.
If no 'file' is specified, the standard input is used.
.HE
.B grep
searches each
.I file
for lines matching
.IR pattern ,
a regular expression given in the same syntax as
.BR ed .
If no
.I file
is given, it searches the standard input.
Normally each matching line is printed; several options change what
is printed and how the match is made.
.PP
More than one
.I pattern
may be given, with repeated
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
options (or one of each); a line is a match if it matches any of
them.
If no
.B \-e
or
.B \-f
option is given, the first non-option argument is taken as
.I pattern
instead.
.SH Patterns
Ordinary characters in
.I pattern
match themselves.
Without
.BR \-a ,
the following are also recognized:
.IP \fB^\fR 0.4i
Anchors the match to the start of the line.
.IP \fB$\fR
Anchors the match to the end of the line.
.IP \fB.\fR
Matches any single character.
.IP \fB*\fR
Matches zero or more occurrences of the preceding pattern element.
.IP \fB[\fIchars\fB]\fR
Matches any one of the enclosed characters; a leading
.B ^
complements the set, and ranges may be written with
.BR \- .
.IP \fB\e\fIc\fR
Matches the character
.I c
literally.
.PP
With
.BR \-a ,
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR | ,
.BR + ,
and
.B ?
are also recognized, with the same meaning as in
.BR egrep .
With
.BR \-x ,
none of the above are special: the whole
.I pattern
must equal the whole line, character for character.
.SH Options
.IP \fB\-a\fR 0.4i
Accept the extended metacharacters
.BR ( ,
.BR ) ,
.BR | ,
.BR + ,
and
.B ?
in
.IR pattern ,
as
.B egrep
does.
.IP \fB\-b\fR
Precede each matching line with the number of the input block it
started in.
.IP \fB\-c\fR
Print only a count of matching lines, not the lines themselves.
.IP \fB\-e\fR
The next argument is
.IR pattern ,
useful when it begins with
.BR \- .
.IP \fB\-f\fR
The next argument names a file containing one pattern per line.
.IP \fB\-h\fR
Do not print file names ahead of matched lines.
.IP \fB\-l\fR
Print only the names of files that contain a match.
.IP \fB\-n\fR
Precede each matching line with its line number within its file.
.IP \fB\-s\fR
Print nothing; only set the exit status.
.IP \fB\-v\fR
Print lines that do
.I not
match any
.IR pattern .
.IP \fB\-x\fR
Require the whole line to equal
.I pattern
exactly; metacharacters in
.I pattern
are treated as ordinary text.
.IP \fB\-y\fR
Make lower-case letters in
.I pattern
match either case in the input.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR awk ,
.BR ed ,
.B egrep
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B grep
returns 0 if any line matched, 1 if none did, and 2 if invoked
incorrectly or a file could not be opened.
.SH NOTES
.B egrep
is an extended and faster version of
.BR grep .
